+# Project note — Experiment A task-set effect
+
+**Status:** unresolved observation; non-normative
+**Source:** V2.10 Dijkstra production report
+**Return condition:** revisit only if later evidence suggests explanatory value
+
+## Observation
+
+The frozen Experiment A task set produced materially different mean paired
+expansion effects by task template. The single `medium_diagonal` probe had the
+largest negative mean log expansion ratio:
+
+\[
+\overline{\Delta}_{\text{medium diagonal}}
+\approx -0.0747.
+\]
+
+The overall eight-probe production estimate was approximately:
+
+\[
+\overline{\Delta}_{\text{all probes}}
+\approx -0.0195.
+\]
+
+Task variation was much larger than between-mechanism variation in the V2.10
+variance decomposition.
+
+## Why this is not yet a finding about diagonal tasks
+
+The task bank was deliberately structured:
+
+- one task per named category;
+- normalized \(\mathcal Q\)-space endpoints;
+- midpoints concentrated near the center of the output box;
+- mostly positive movement in both joint coordinates;
+- fixed point-to-point configuration goals;
+- four-connected grid;
+- actuator-travel cost;
+- start-rooted Dijkstra expansion count.
+
+The category effect therefore entangles:
+
+- displacement direction;
+- displacement length;
+- start location;
+- goal location;
+- passage through the central branch region;
+- local actuator gain;
+- Dijkstra cost-ball geometry;
+- grid and tie semantics.
+
+Calling this an "efficient diagonal area" would exceed the evidence.
+
+## Plausible interpretations
+
+The observation may be useful if it reflects:
+
+- alignment between the diagonal displacement and a favorable actuator-metric
+  corridor;
+- complementary transmission weighting across the two axes;
+- reduced low-cost expansion basin for that start-goal chord;
+- a repeatable task-direction effect that survives relocation and reversal.
+
+It may be metrics slop if it reflects:
+
+- one idiosyncratic canonical task;
+- four-connected lattice anisotropy;
+- deterministic tie-breaking;
+- start-versus-goal asymmetry in settled-node count;
+- a feature specific to fixed \(\mathcal Q\) endpoints that disappears under a
+  Cartesian goal set;
+- a finite-resolution interaction.
+
+## Do not expand scope now
+
+Do not add a task atlas to Experiment B's primary scope. Experiment B should use
+its frozen external Cartesian distribution without being tuned around this
+observation.
+
+## Efficient return path
+
+Revisit this note if either:
+
+1. the same-bank A* campaign shows a consistent task-template interaction; or
+2. Experiment B shows a directional or regional Cartesian effect that could be
+   connected to the Experiment A probe.
+
+The smallest useful follow-up is:
+
+1. reverse each canonical task;
+2. translate a fixed displacement vector across feasible \(\mathcal Q\)
+   midpoints;
+3. separate midpoint, length, and direction;
+4. compare Dijkstra and A*;
+5. repeat at the accepted and confirmation resolutions.
+
+Only after those controls should a formal task-effect field be considered:
+
+\[
+\Delta
+=
+\Delta(
+\mathbf m_Q,
+\|\mathbf d_Q\|,
+\angle\mathbf d_Q,
+\text{solver}
+).
+\]
+
+Until then, preserve the observation and make no directional mechanism claim.
